---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an experienced professional writer, editor and content strategist.

TASK:
Write a high-quality article or blog post based on the information provided below.

The content should be useful, accurate, well structured, natural to read and appropriate for the intended audience.

Do not invent facts, statistics, research findings, customer results, expert quotes, case studies, product capabilities or sources.

When important information is missing, either:

- write around the uncertainty,
- clearly label an assumption,
- use a placeholder,
- or identify what should be researched before publication.

TOPIC:
[What should the article be about?]

PRIMARY PURPOSE:
[Educate / explain / inform / compare / persuade / generate leads / thought leadership / SEO / other.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who will read it?]

AUDIENCE KNOWLEDGE LEVEL:
[Beginner / intermediate / advanced / mixed.]

MAIN MESSAGE:
[What should readers understand or remember?]

CONTENT TYPE:
[Blog post / article / guide / tutorial / thought-leadership article / list article / comparison / other.]

DESIRED LENGTH:
[Approximate word count or short / medium / long.]

TONE:
[Professional / conversational / authoritative / friendly / practical / technical / other.]

BRAND VOICE:
[Describe the preferred style if applicable.]

PRIMARY KEYWORD:
[Optional.]

SECONDARY KEYWORDS:
[Optional.]

SEARCH INTENT:
[Informational / commercial investigation / transactional / navigational / unknown.]

FACTS / SOURCE MATERIAL:
[Paste verified information, research, notes, product information or source material.]

EXAMPLES / EXPERIENCE:
[Real examples that may be used.]

PRODUCT / SERVICE:
[If relevant.]

CALL TO ACTION:
[What should the reader do next?]

WORDS / CLAIMS TO AVOID:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

ARTICLE CREATION REQUIREMENTS:

1. DEFINE THE READER
Before writing, identify:

Who the article is for
What they probably want to know
What problem they are trying to solve
What level of explanation they need

Do not invent detailed demographic characteristics.

2. DEFINE THE ARTICLE PURPOSE
State the primary purpose in one sentence.

Keep the article focused on that purpose.

3. IDENTIFY SEARCH / READER INTENT
Where relevant determine whether the reader primarily wants to:

Learn
Solve a problem
Compare options
Make a decision
Complete a task
Understand a concept

4. DEFINE THE CORE PROMISE
State what useful outcome the reader should receive from the article.

5. REVIEW AVAILABLE EVIDENCE
Separate information into:

CONFIRMED FACTS
Supported by supplied information or reliable sources.

USER-PROVIDED EXPERIENCE
Real examples or observations supplied by the user.

ASSUMPTIONS
Reasonable but unverified.

UNKNOWN
Information requiring research.

6. NEVER FABRICATE EVIDENCE
Do not invent:

Statistics
Studies
Survey results
Expert quotes
Customer testimonials
Company results
Market size
Dates
Historical events
Product features
Scientific claims

7. HANDLE MISSING FACTS SAFELY
When factual evidence is necessary but unavailable, use a clear marker such as:

[VERIFY THIS CLAIM]

[ADD SOURCE]

[INSERT VERIFIED STATISTIC]

rather than inventing information.

8. IDENTIFY THE MAIN ANGLE
Choose a clear angle appropriate to the audience.

Avoid trying to cover every possible aspect of the topic.

9. CREATE A WORKING TITLE
Generate a clear, specific title.

Avoid exaggerated clickbait unless explicitly requested.

10. CREATE ALTERNATIVE TITLES
Where useful provide 3–5 alternatives emphasizing different angles.

11. WRITE A STRONG INTRODUCTION
The introduction should quickly establish:

The problem
Why it matters
What the reader will learn

Avoid long generic introductions.

12. AVOID GENERIC AI OPENINGS
Avoid repetitive openings such as:

"In today's fast-paced world..."

"In the ever-evolving landscape..."

"In this digital age..."

unless genuinely appropriate.

13. GET TO THE POINT
Deliver useful information early.

Do not spend several paragraphs repeating the title.

14. CREATE A LOGICAL STRUCTURE
Use:

H1 title
H2 main sections
H3 subsections where useful

15. BUILD A CLEAR INFORMATION FLOW
Prefer:

Problem
Explanation
Method
Example
Action

or another structure appropriate to the topic.

16. USE DESCRIPTIVE HEADINGS
Headings should tell readers what the section contains.

Prefer:

"How to Calculate Reorder Point"

over:

"Understanding the Process"

17. MAKE EACH SECTION USEFUL
Each section should contribute:

Explanation
Evidence
Example
Instruction
Decision support

Avoid filler.

18. USE SHORTER PARAGRAPHS
Keep paragraphs readable for web content.

Do not turn every sentence into a separate paragraph.

19. VARY SENTENCE LENGTH
Use natural rhythm.

Avoid a repetitive pattern of similarly sized sentences.

20. USE PLAIN LANGUAGE
Prefer clear language over unnecessary complexity.

21. PRESERVE NECESSARY TECHNICAL TERMS
Do not oversimplify terminology the audience needs.

Explain unfamiliar terms where appropriate.

22. DEFINE ACRONYMS
On first use write the full term followed by the acronym where appropriate.

23. AVOID UNNECESSARY JARGON
Replace corporate or technical jargon when simpler wording communicates the same meaning.

24. AVOID EMPTY BUSINESS LANGUAGE
Reduce phrases such as:

Leverage synergies
Unlock potential
Drive transformation
Revolutionary solution

unless they communicate something specific.

25. USE ACTIVE VOICE WHERE NATURAL
Prefer direct writing.

Do not mechanically eliminate passive voice when it improves clarity.

26. USE TRANSITIONS NATURALLY
Connect ideas without overusing:

Furthermore
Moreover
Additionally
In conclusion

27. AVOID REPETITION
Do not repeat the same point using slightly different wording merely to increase length.

28. USE EXAMPLES
Include examples when they improve understanding.

Only use:

Supplied real examples
Clearly labeled hypothetical examples

29. LABEL HYPOTHETICAL EXAMPLES
If creating an illustration, make clear it is hypothetical.

30. USE STEP-BY-STEP INSTRUCTIONS
For procedural content, organize actions in the correct sequence.

31. DISTINGUISH INSTRUCTION FROM EXPLANATION
Readers should be able to identify:

What to do
Why it matters

32. INCLUDE FORMULAS WHEN RELEVANT
Present formulas clearly.

Define every variable.

33. SHOW CALCULATIONS WHEN RELEVANT
Use supplied values or clearly labeled hypothetical examples.

34. USE TABLES WHEN THEY IMPROVE COMPARISON
Tables may be useful for:

Options
Features
Pros and cons
Processes
Calculations

Do not create tables unnecessarily.

35. USE BULLETS SELECTIVELY
Use lists for genuinely list-like information.

Do not convert the entire article into bullet points.

36. SUPPORT IMPORTANT CLAIMS
Identify statements that require external evidence.

Do not make authoritative claims without support.

37. DISTINGUISH FACT FROM OPINION
Clearly signal recommendations, interpretations and judgment.

38. HANDLE UNCERTAINTY
Use wording such as:

May
Can
Often
Depends on
Based on available information

when certainty is not justified.

39. DO NOT OVERSTATE BENEFITS
Avoid claims such as:

Guaranteed
Always
Best
Perfect
Eliminates all risk

unless objectively supported.

40. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT BENEFITS
When discussing a product or service, use only verified capabilities.

41. ADDRESS THE READER'S MAIN QUESTIONS
Anticipate practical questions the target reader is likely to have based on the topic.

Do not fabricate audience research.

42. EXPLAIN WHY
When giving recommendations, explain the reasoning.

43. IDENTIFY COMMON MISTAKES
Where useful explain realistic mistakes readers should avoid.

Do not invent statistics about how common they are.

44. INCLUDE LIMITATIONS
Explain important situations where the advice may not apply.

45. PRESENT ALTERNATIVES
If there are several legitimate approaches, explain them rather than pretending one method is universally correct.

46. INCLUDE PROS AND CONS
For decision-oriented articles, present meaningful trade-offs.

47. AVOID FALSE BALANCE
Do not create artificial advantages or disadvantages merely to make both sides appear equal.

48. KEEP THE AUDIENCE LEVEL CONSISTENT
Do not suddenly shift from beginner explanations to unexplained specialist terminology.

49. MAKE THE CONTENT ACTIONABLE
Whenever possible help the reader understand:

What to do next
What to check
What to avoid
What decision to make

50. CREATE PRACTICAL TAKEAWAYS
End important sections with a clear takeaway when useful.

51. SEO — USE THE PRIMARY KEYWORD NATURALLY
If a keyword is supplied, consider using it in:

Title
Introduction
At least one heading
Body
Conclusion

Only where natural.

52. DO NOT KEYWORD STUFF
Readability and usefulness take priority over repeating keywords.

53. USE RELATED TERMS NATURALLY
Use relevant terminology that improves topical clarity.

54. MATCH SEARCH INTENT
Do not write a sales page when the reader is looking for an educational explanation.

55. ANSWER THE MAIN QUESTION EARLY
For informational search content, provide a useful direct answer near the beginning where appropriate.

56. OPTIMIZE FOR SCANNABILITY
Use:

Descriptive headings
Short paragraphs
Selective lists
Tables where useful
Bold emphasis sparingly

57. CREATE A META TITLE
Where SEO is requested, provide an SEO-friendly title.

Keep it clear and accurate rather than clickbait-heavy.

58. CREATE A META DESCRIPTION
Summarize the article's value accurately.

Avoid unsupported promises.

59. SUGGEST A URL SLUG
Use a concise, descriptive slug.

Example:

/inventory-reorder-point-guide/

60. SUGGEST INTERNAL LINKS
Where website context is supplied, identify relevant internal pages that could naturally be linked.

Do not invent URLs.

61. SUGGEST EXTERNAL SOURCE NEEDS
Identify claims that should link to authoritative external evidence.

Do not fabricate citations.

62. FEATURED SNIPPET OPPORTUNITY
Where appropriate provide a concise definition or answer that can stand alone.

63. FAQ SECTION
Only include FAQs when they add genuine reader value.

Do not add repetitive questions just for SEO.

64. USE NATURAL QUESTIONS
FAQ questions should reflect realistic information needs.

65. E-E-A-T SUPPORT
Where relevant strengthen credibility through:

Real experience
Clear methodology
Verified evidence
Transparent limitations
Author expertise

Do not manufacture credentials or experience.

66. USE FIRST-HAND EXPERIENCE CAREFULLY
Only write "I tested," "we found," or similar language if the user actually supplied that experience.

67. AVOID FAKE QUOTES
Never create fictional expert or customer quotations presented as real.

68. AVOID FAKE CASE STUDIES
Hypothetical scenarios must be labeled clearly.

69. DO NOT COPY SOURCE LANGUAGE
When source material is supplied, synthesize it in original language unless a direct quotation is specifically needed.

70. PRESERVE TECHNICAL ACCURACY
Do not simplify information in a way that changes its meaning.

71. HANDLE SAFETY-SENSITIVE CONTENT CAREFULLY
For medical, legal, financial, safety or similarly high-stakes subjects, avoid presenting general writing as individualized professional advice.

72. HANDLE CURRENT INFORMATION
If the topic depends on current:

Prices
Laws
Statistics
Product specifications
Software features
Market information
News

identify where current verification is necessary.

73. CREATE ORIGINAL VALUE
Do more than summarize basic information.

Where appropriate add:

Frameworks
Decision criteria
Checklists
Examples
Practical explanations
Common mistakes

74. AVOID CONTENT PADDING
Do not increase word count through repetition.

If the useful answer is shorter than requested, prioritize quality.

75. MAINTAIN BRAND VOICE
If brand guidance is supplied, keep tone and terminology consistent.

76. KEEP PROMOTIONAL CONTENT PROPORTIONAL
If the article supports a business, educate first.

Do not turn every section into a sales pitch.

77. PLACE PRODUCT MENTIONS NATURALLY
Mention the product or service where it genuinely helps solve the reader's problem.

78. WRITE AN APPROPRIATE CTA
The CTA should logically follow the article.

Examples:

Try a tool
Download a resource
Read a related guide
Request information
Compare options

79. AVOID MANIPULATIVE CTAs
Do not use unnecessary fear, false urgency or unsupported scarcity.

80. WRITE A USEFUL CONCLUSION
Summarize:

Main lesson
Important decision or action
Logical next step

Do not simply repeat the introduction.

81. PROVIDE A QUICK-READ SUMMARY
For longer articles, provide 3–5 key takeaways where useful.

82. IDENTIFY CONTENT GAPS
After drafting, identify important questions the article could not answer because evidence was unavailable.

83. FACT-CHECK REVIEW
Create a list of statements requiring verification before publication.

84. SOURCE CHECK
Identify where sources are needed.

Do not invent source names.

85. CLAIM REVIEW
Flag:

Numerical claims
Comparative claims
Scientific claims
Legal claims
Market claims
Performance claims

that require evidence.

86. REMOVE AI-SOUNDING REPETITION
Review for:

Repeated sentence structures
Unnecessary summaries
Generic transitions
Overly polished corporate wording
Predictable three-item phrasing
Excessive em dashes

Rewrite where needed.

87. HUMANIZE THE RHYTHM
Use natural variation while preserving professionalism.

88. REMOVE EMPTY INTRODUCTORY PHRASES
Delete sentences that do not add information.

89. CHECK PARAGRAPH PURPOSE
Every paragraph should contribute something meaningful.

90. CHECK HEADING LOGIC
Ensure headings form a coherent outline even when read without the body text.

91. CHECK FOR CONTRADICTIONS
Verify that later sections do not conflict with earlier recommendations.

92. CHECK TERMINOLOGY
Use the same term consistently for the same concept.

93. CHECK NUMBERS
Ensure supplied numbers are reproduced accurately.

94. CHECK UNITS
Preserve correct:

Currency
Weight
Distance
Time
Percentages
Measurement units

95. CHECK CALL TO ACTION
Ensure the CTA matches the article's purpose.

96. CREATE PUBLICATION ELEMENTS
Where requested provide:

Article title
Meta title
Meta description
URL slug
Excerpt
Social caption

97. CREATE AN EDITOR'S NOTE
List anything that should be:

Verified
Updated
Sourced
Approved

before publication.

98. PROVIDE OPTIONAL IMPROVEMENTS
Suggest missing evidence, examples, visuals or internal links that would materially improve the article.

99. CONFIDENCE CHECK
State whether factual confidence is:

High
Medium
Low

based on the information supplied.

Do not confuse writing quality with factual confidence.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- the article answers the reader's main need
- the introduction gets to the point
- structure is logical
- headings are descriptive
- paragraphs are readable
- claims are supported or flagged
- no statistics were invented
- no quotes were invented
- no sources were invented
- no fake experience was added
- hypothetical examples are labeled
- keywords are natural
- repetition is removed
- tone fits the audience
- conclusion provides a useful next step
- factual gaps are visible

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. Recommended Title
2. Alternative Titles
3. Article
4. Key Takeaways — if useful
5. FAQ — if useful
6. Call to Action
7. SEO Meta Title — if requested
8. Meta Description — if requested
9. Suggested URL Slug — if requested
10. Internal Link Opportunities — if website context is supplied
11. Claims / Facts Requiring Verification
12. Recommended Source Needs
13. Editor's Notes
14. Confidence Level

IMPORTANT:
- Never invent facts, statistics, studies, quotes, customer results, product capabilities or citations.
- Label hypothetical examples clearly.
- Separate confirmed information from assumptions.
- Match the reader's actual intent.
- Prioritize useful information over word count.
- Avoid generic AI-style introductions and repetitive filler.
- Use SEO keywords naturally rather than stuffing them.
- Do not manufacture personal experience or expertise.
- Identify claims requiring verification before publication.
- Keep the writing natural, specific, practical and appropriate for the intended audience.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Define the reader and purpose",
    description:
      "Tell the AI who the article is for, what those readers need and what the content should help them understand, decide or accomplish.",
  },
  {
    title: "Provide real source material",
    description:
      "Add your verified facts, notes, experience, product information or research so the article can contain genuine substance instead of fabricated evidence.",
  },
  {
    title: "Build for humans first",
    description:
      "Use clear headings, practical explanations and natural language. SEO should support the article rather than control how every sentence is written.",
  },
  {
    title: "Verify before publishing",
    description:
      "Review statistics, comparisons, current information and other factual claims flagged by the prompt before publishing the final article.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Topic: Why physical inventory may not match system stock.",
    "Audience: Small warehouse supervisors and inventory controllers.",
    "Purpose: Educational article.",
    "Tone: Practical and professional.",
    "Main points: Receiving errors, picking errors, incorrect system transactions, damaged stock, wrong locations and stock-count errors.",
    "Requirement: Do not invent statistics.",
  ],

  output: [
    "Recommended angle: Explain the most common process areas to investigate when physical stock and system stock do not agree.",
    "Introduction: Start with the practical problem and explain that a stock variance is usually a symptom that requires transaction and process investigation.",
    "Structure: Receiving → putaway/location → picking → system transactions → damage/adjustments → stock counting.",
    "Practical value: Give readers a simple investigation sequence instead of only listing possible causes.",
    "Evidence handling: Do not claim that any one cause is 'the most common' unless reliable supporting data is available.",
    "Conclusion: Encourage correction of both the quantity discrepancy and the underlying process or control failure that caused it.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Give the AI evidence, not just a topic",
    description:
      "Notes, verified facts, real experience and source material make it much easier to create useful original content without relying on generic or invented claims.",
  },
  {
    title: "Write for intent before keywords",
    description:
      "A page performs its real job when it answers what the reader came to learn. Keywords should fit naturally around that answer.",
  },
  {
    title: "Treat factual confidence separately",
    description:
      "An article can sound excellent while containing weak claims. Always verify statistics, current facts, comparisons and specialist claims before publication.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Article & Blog Writer Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create structured, natural and useful articles or blog posts while avoiding fabricated facts, citations, statistics and unnecessary AI-style filler."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="WRITING"
    title="Article & Blog"
    accentTitle="Writer."
    description="Create useful, well-structured articles and blog posts for real readers while keeping facts, SEO, tone and source requirements under control."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Content writing",
      "SEO friendly",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Bloggers",
      "Businesses",
      "Website owners",
      "Content marketers",
      "Professional articles",
      "Educational content",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Create useful content instead of generic AI articles."
    introDescription="Give the AI a real audience, purpose and evidence base, then build an article around clear explanations, practical value and claims that can actually be supported."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn professional knowledge into a useful article."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Make AI-assisted articles worth publishing."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Already have a draft?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Rewrite for Clarity, Professional Tone Rewriter or Humanize AI-Written Text prompts when the content already exists and needs editing rather than a complete new article."
    warningTitle="Good writing does not guarantee factual accuracy"
    warningText="AI can produce convincing unsupported statements. Verify statistics, current information, comparative claims, technical facts and specialist advice before publishing, and never use fabricated citations or experience."
  />
</BaseLayout>